**OxDock is a Dockerfile-inspired DSL for native builds, multi-node pipelines, and sealed remote execution.**

Bash gives you strings and exit codes. OxDock gives you [typed values]({{ $global.workspace_url }}#value-types), [guarded paths]({{ $global.workspace_url }}#workspaces--filesystem), [managed pipes]({{ $global.workspace_url }}#transport-pipes), and identical behavior on Linux/macOS/Windows (the things you'd re-implement badly in every growing bash script). And with hot-loading [MCP tooling]({{ $global.workspace_url }}#mcp-hosting), *English-to-OxDock* just skips the boilerplate of getting there. [Workspaces]({{ $global.workspace_url }}#workspaces--filesystem) (`SNAPSHOT`, `LOCAL`, `CACHE`, `SYSTEM`) and [scoped blocks]({{ $global.workspace_url }}#guards-and-scoped-blocks) with [selective environment inheritance]({{ $global.workspace_url }}#selective-environment-inheritance) give every run an isolated root that reverts on scope exit. When plain shell is the right tool, [`RUN`]({{ $global.workspace_url }}#run) still reaches it, and [platform guards]({{ $global.workspace_url }}#platform-guards) keep the OS-specific bits fenced: the two combine instead of competing.

Unlike Docker's Linux-VM requirement on non-Linux hosts, OxDock runs directly on Linux, macOS, and Windows. Execute scripts on bare metal, inside containers, or dispatched across remote machines simultaneously. No daemons. No hypervisors.

Platform gating selects OS-specific steps, and remote targets bind over any stdio transport. Only RUN touches the host shell.

The syntax is line-oriented with optional semicolon terminators and a static type system checking scripts before the first step runs. Plain Rust functions become script functions with one attribute: `#[oxdock_func]` exports them into namespaced modules scripts call as `DEMO::NAME(...)`. See [Extending OxDock from Rust](#extending-oxdock-from-rust).

[Documentation](https://docs.rs/oxdock/{{ env:CRATE_VERSION }}/oxdock/)
